{"timestamp":"2026-10-04T16:40:02.254Z","ordinal":15099,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","turn_id":"01a1055e-488d-75e2-ab79-097882eda37b","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-243c554d-6fc4-4e04-8101-be23cd9c2192","process_id":"58576","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '38,157p' node_modules/alchemy/src/Platform.ts; sed -n '307,357p' node_modules/alchemy/src/Platform.ts; sed -n '500,555p' node_modules/alchemy/src/Platform.ts; sed -n '2200,2246p' node_modules/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Workers/Worker.ts; sed -n '1,210p' .agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/architecture/ownership-and-boundaries.md; sed -n '1,200p' .agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/providers/cloudflare.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees/aac7/taxkit","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '38,157p' node_modules/alchemy/src/Platform.ts","name":"Platform.ts","path":"node_modules/alchemy/src/Platform.ts"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '307,357p' node_modules/alchemy/src/Platform.ts","name":"Platform.ts","path":"node_modules/alchemy/src/Platform.ts"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '500,555p' node_modules/alchemy/src/Platform.ts","name":"Platform.ts","path":"node_modules/alchemy/src/Platform.ts"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '2200,2246p' node_modules/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Workers/Worker.ts","name":"Worker.ts","path":"node_modules/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Workers/Worker.ts"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,210p' .agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/architecture/ownership-and-boundaries.md","name":"ownership-and-boundaries.md","path":".agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/architecture/ownership-and-boundaries.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,200p' .agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/providers/cloudflare.md","name":"cloudflare.md","path":".agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/providers/cloudflare.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"export interface PlatformProps {\n  /**\n   * @internal type used to signal when this is an effect-native implementation\n   * @default false\n   */\n  isExternal?: boolean;\n}\n\n/**\n * Provide the platform class's layer (`cls.make(props, impl)`) with a\n * lifetime that matches the phase.\n *\n * **At runtime** (`__ALCHEMY_RUNTIME__`, folded to `true` in every bundled\n * artifact) the layer builds against the AMBIENT scope. `Effect.provide` is\n * implemented with `scopedWith`, so its transient region scope would tear\n * the layer down — firing init-level finalizers and releasing\n * `Layer.scoped` services — the moment init completes. The runtime bridges\n * evaluate the entrypoint under the instance-lifetime build scope (closed\n * at instance shutdown where the platform offers one — Lambda's SIGTERM\n * window — and never on workerd), so building against it keeps instance\n * services alive for the instance.\n *\n * **At plan/deploy** it stays `Effect.provide`: the transient region evicts\n * the layer's memo entry when each `yield*` of the class completes, so\n * every deploy in a session re-evaluates the resource and re-registers its\n * Output sources. Building on the session scope would keep the memo alive\n * across deploys and a second `stack.deploy` would skip source\n * registration (`MissingSourceError`).\n */\nconst provideClassLayer =\n  <ROut, E2, RIn>(layer: Layer.Layer<ROut, E2, RIn>) =>\n  <A, E, R>(\n    self: Effect.Effect<A, E, R>,\n  ): Effect.Effect<A, E | E2, RIn | Scope | Exclude<R, ROut>> =>\n    (globalThis.__ALCHEMY_RUNTIME__\n      ? Effect.flatMap(Effect.scope, (scope) =>\n          Effect.flatMap(Layer.buildWithScope(layer, scope), (context) =>\n            Effect.provideContext(self, context),\n          ),\n        )\n      : Effect.provide(self, layer)) as Effect.Effect<\n      A,\n      E | E2,\n      RIn | Scope | Exclude<R, ROut>\n    >;\n\nexport type Main<InitServices = never> = void | {\n  fetch?:\n    | HttpEffect<InitServices | PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope>\n    | Effect.Effect<\n        HttpEffect<InitServices | PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope>,\n        never,\n        InitServices | PlatformServices\n      >;\n};\n\nexport interface MainRpc<Req = never> {\n  [key: string]:\n    | Effect.Effect<\n        any,\n        any,\n        PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | HttpServerRequest | Scope | Req\n      >\n    | Stream.Stream<\n        any,\n        any,\n        PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | HttpServerRequest | Scope | Req\n      >\n    | ((\n        ...args: any[]\n      ) =>\n        | Effect.Effect<\n            any,\n            any,\n            PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope | Req\n          >\n        | Stream.Stream<\n            any,\n            any,\n            PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope | Req\n          >);\n}\n\n// Strip `void`/`undefined`/`never` from `Shape` before intersecting it with\n// `BaseShape`. This matters when `Shape` fails its `extends MainShape`\n// constraint (e.g. a `fetch` handler that leaks an error): TS clamps `Shape`\n// to the constraint union `void | { fetch: ... }`, and a *distributive*\n// conditional would split that into `BaseShape | ({ fetch } & BaseShape)` — a\n// union. Feeding a union into the `new (_: never): ...` construct signature\n// makes the base class a union type, which surfaces as the cryptic\n// ts(2509) \"Base constructor return type ... is not an object type\" instead of\n// the real assignability error on the `impl` argument. Excluding `void` here\n// keeps the construct-sig return a single object type, so only the actionable\n// error remains.\nexport type MakeShape<Shape, BaseShape> = [\n  Exclude<Shape, void | undefined>,\n] extends [never]\n  ? Exclude<BaseShape, void | undefined>\n  : Exclude<Shape, void | undefined> & Exclude<BaseShape, void | undefined>;\n\n// Services provided to the Resource's init/props effects. Deliberately does\n// NOT include `Scope`: init runs once per instance under a build scope that\n// closes at instance shutdown at best (Lambda's SIGTERM window; never on\n// workerd), so init code that needs a scope must not typecheck. Handlers get\n// a fresh per-event `Scope` from the bridge — note the explicit `| Scope` on\n// the handler positions in `Main` / `MainRpc` above.\nexport type PlatformServices =\n  | NodeServices\n  | HttpClient\n  | Provider<any>\n  | ProviderCollectionLike\n  | Stack\n  | StackServices\n  | Stage;\n\n/** A platform declaration's logical identity, readable without yielding it. */\nexport interface PlatformIdentity<Id extends string = string> {\n  readonly LogicalId: Id;\n}\n\n    createRuntimeContext: (id: string) => BaseRuntimeContext;\n    /**\n     * Legacy type names this platform's resource was previously registered\n     * under (see `ResourceOptions.aliases`) — threaded to the underlying\n     * `Resource` so state persisted under a pre-rename type keeps\n     * resolving.\n     */\n    aliases?: string[];\n    // `onCreate` runs inside the resource-construction context, which already\n    // carries the Stack's providers — so the hook may yield child resources\n    // (e.g. an async Worker registering a `WorkflowResource` for a bound\n    // Workflow). Allow an ambient requirement (`any`) rather than forcing\n    // `never`; it is discharged by the surrounding provider context.\n    onCreate?: (resource: R, props: any) => Effect.Effect<void, never, any>;\n    /**\n     * Transform the user-supplied props before the underlying resource is\n     * declared. Runs in the resource-construction context (Stack, Namespace,\n     * and providers are available), so the hook may compose REAL child\n     * resources (the `StaticSite` pattern) and return derived props that\n     * reference their Outputs. Implementations MUST be a no-op at runtime\n     * (guard on `globalThis.__ALCHEMY_RUNTIME__`) — the hook is evaluated\n     * wherever the props effect is, including inside deployed bundles.\n     */\n    transformProps?: (id: string, props: any) => Effect.Effect<any, any, any>;\n  },\n  methods?: { [key: string]: any },\n): any => {\n  type Props = any;\n  type Impl = Effect.Effect<any>;\n\n  // Platform registrations must have resolved props by plan time: every\n  // legitimate construction (a `.make(props, impl)` Layer build, a tag\n  // declared with props, a plain call) produces them, so props still\n  // `undefined` at plan can only be a bare-tag forward reference whose\n  // `.make` Layer was never provided — `Plan.make` fails fast naming the\n  // class and its Layer (#1054).\n  const resource = Resource(type, {\n    aliases: hooks.aliases,\n    requiresImplementation: true,\n  });\n  const PlatformContext = RuntimeContext;\n\n  // Apply the optional `transformProps` hook to a (possibly Effect-valued)\n  // props argument. Returns the props untouched when no hook is installed so\n  // the plain-object fast paths below keep working.\n  const applyTransformProps = (id: string, props: any): any =>\n    hooks.transformProps === undefined\n      ? props\n      : Effect.flatMap(\n          Effect.isEffect(props)\n            ? (props as Effect.Effect<any>)\n          Self,\n          Effect.flatMap(\n            Effect.all([\n              (() => {\n                const transformed = applyTransformProps(id, props);\n                return Effect.isEffect(transformed)\n                  ? (transformed as Effect.Effect<any>)\n                  : Effect.succeed(transformed ?? {});\n              })(),\n              Effect.sync(() => hooks.createRuntimeContext(id)),\n              Effect.context<never>(),\n            ]),\n            Effect.fn(function* ([props, runtimeContext, outerServices]) {\n              // The init effect (`impl`) is evaluated inside an\n              // `Effect.provide(...)` region below, whose implementation\n              // (`scopedWith`) would otherwise shadow the ambient `Scope`\n              // with a transient one that closes the moment init returns.\n              // Pin init's ambient scope to this layer's build scope\n              // instead: under the runtime bridges that scope belongs to\n              // the instance-lifetime build, so init-level finalizers run\n              // at instance shutdown or not at all — never per event\n              // (workerd never closes it; the Lambda entry closes it in the\n              // SIGTERM window). Request-coupled cleanup belongs in\n              // handlers, where the bridge provides a per-event scope.\n              const buildScope = yield* Effect.scope;\n              const instance = Object.assign(\n                yield* resource(id, props as any).pipe(\n                  Effect.flatMap(\n                    (resource) =>\n                      hooks\n                        .onCreate?.(resource, props)\n                        .pipe(Effect.map(() => resource)) ??\n                      Effect.succeed(resource),\n                  ),\n                ),\n                runtimeContext,\n              );\n\n              yield* impl.pipe(\n                Effect.flatMap((impl) => {\n                  if (!impl) return Effect.void;\n                  const shape = impl as Record<string, unknown>;\n                  // Serve when there's a `fetch` handler OR any RPC shape\n                  // methods. A pure-RPC impl (methods, no `fetch`) still needs\n                  // the server to boot — hand `serveRpc` a default 404 fallback\n                  // so `/__rpc__/*` is dispatched to the shape methods and\n                  // everything else 404s.\n                  // May be an `HttpEffect` or an Effect resolving to one (the\n                  // `Main.fetch` shape); `serve` accepts both.\n                  const fetch = shape.fetch as any;\n                  const hasRpcMethods = Object.keys(shape).some(\n                    (key) => key !== \"fetch\",\n                  );\n                  if (!fetch && !hasRpcMethods) return Effect.void;\n                  // Hand the full impl to `serve` so the runtime can expose any\n                  // non-handler methods on the impl shape (RPC methods)\n * **Example:** Enabling Workers Cache on an async Worker\n * ```typescript\n * {\n *   main: \"./src/worker.ts\",\n *   cache: {\n *     enabled: true,\n *     crossVersionCache: true,\n *   },\n * }\n * ```\n *\n * ### Background Work & Scopes\n * Each incoming event (fetch, RPC call, scheduled run) gets its own Effect\n * `Scope`. When the handler finishes, the bridge closes that scope and\n * registers the close promise with workerd's `ctx.waitUntil` — so\n * finalizers added with `Effect.addFinalizer` inside a handler run *after*\n * the response is sent, without blocking it, and the Worker stays alive\n * until they settle. Streaming responses transfer the scope to the stream,\n * so those finalizers run when the stream completes instead.\n *\n * For ad-hoc background work, `WorkerExecutionContext.waitUntil(effect)`\n * forks an Effect with the caller's full context and keeps the invocation\n * alive until it settles. The context can be yielded once in the\n * constructor and used from any handler; its methods are `RuntimeContext`-\n * colored, so they can only run inside a handler.\n *\n * The constructor is evaluated once per isolate: the bridge builds the\n * Worker's layer stack on the first event and every later event reuses the\n * built services. Resolve services, bind resources, build handlers there —\n * one-shot I/O that caches a plain value (e.g. fetching a secret for a\n * client) is fine, but nothing disposable: the build scope is never closed\n * (workerd has no isolate-teardown hook), so a finalizer added in the\n * constructor never runs, and I/O-backed objects (sockets, response bodies) are\n * pinned to the request that created them. Anything that needs cleanup\n * belongs in a handler, where `Effect.addFinalizer` attaches to the\n * per-event scope.\n *\n * **Example:** Post-response cleanup with a scope finalizer\n * ```typescript\n * return {\n *   fetch: Effect.gen(function* () {\n *     // runs after this response is sent, kept alive by waitUntil\n *     yield* Effect.addFinalizer(() => flushMetrics().pipe(Effect.ignore));\n *     return HttpServerResponse.text(\"ok\");\n *   }),\n * };\n * ```\n# Ownership and Boundaries\n\n## Contents\n\n- [Start with lifecycle ownership](#start-with-lifecycle-ownership)\n- [Write an ownership matrix](#write-an-ownership-matrix)\n- [Decide when Alchemy should own a resource](#decide-when-alchemy-should-own-a-resource)\n- [Separate authority from capability](#separate-authority-from-capability)\n- [Separate claims](#separate-claims)\n- [Bound custom configuration of foreign resources](#bound-custom-configuration-of-foreign-resources)\n\n## Start with lifecycle ownership\n\nDo not begin with provider APIs. Begin with a resource inventory and name the system that owns each lifecycle transition.\n\nUse these lifecycle verbs precisely:\n\n- **declare**: describe desired state without claiming mutation;\n- **create**: make a resource that does not exist;\n- **adopt**: attach state to a pre-existing resource;\n- **observe**: read and report without changing;\n- **configure**: mutate a bounded setting on a resource owned elsewhere;\n- **reconcile**: converge the managed fields to desired state;\n- **deploy**: create a runtime or artifact from a source revision;\n- **retain**: remove the resource from this stack without deleting it;\n- **destroy**: delete the provider resource and verify absence.\n\nDo not collapse these verbs into “manage”.\n\n## Write an ownership matrix\n\nBefore implementation, record:\n\n| Resource            | Lifecycle owner                   | Deployment owner | State owner   | Readback authority | Removal policy |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------- | ------------------ | -------------- |\n| Cloudflare Worker   | Alchemy or Vercel Git, choose one | named workflow   | named backend | Cloudflare API     | delete/retain  |\n| Vercel deployment   | Vercel Git by default             | Vercel Git       | Vercel        | Vercel API         | Vercel policy  |\n| Axiom dataset       | Alchemy                           | not applicable   | Alchemy state | Axiom API/query    | retain/delete  |\n| WorkOS redirect URI | WorkOS or narrow custom resource  | not applicable   | explicit      | WorkOS API         | delete/retain  |\n\nAdd account, organisation, project, tenant, zone, or team identifiers when ambiguity is possible. Store safe identifiers, never credentials.\n\n## Decide when Alchemy should own a resource\n\nUse Alchemy when all relevant conditions hold:\n\n- the repository owns the desired state;\n- the lifecycle can be expressed deterministically;\n- independent readback exists;\n- create/update/delete semantics are understood;\n- retries can converge;\n- partial failure can be recovered;\n- stage identity can prevent collisions;\n- credentials can be scoped to the authorised operation;\n- the repository can test the graph and the provider result.\n\nPrefer another owner or no new IaC when:\n\n- Vercel Git already owns deployments and source provenance;\n- a resource is manually governed for a documented reason;\n- the provider API cannot support safe read, update, or delete semantics;\n- the only available credential is materially over-broad;\n- the repository cannot distinguish its resource from a foreign one;\n- live state cannot be read independently;\n- the proposed module exists only to make the tree look symmetrical.\n\nAn explicit “no Alchemy role” architecture decision is stronger than an unused scaffold.\n\n## Separate authority from capability\n\nA credential that can mutate a provider does not authorise every mutation it permits.\n\nRequire an operation-specific authority record:\n\n- operation: plan, create, update, adopt, reconcile, destroy, issue token;\n- stage and environment;\n- exact resource set;\n- expected source revision;\n- credential provenance;\n- allowed replacement/deletion;\n- expiry or single-use constraint where appropriate;\n- required preflight and readback;\n- rollback or recovery owner.\n\nKeep provider discovery and read-only diagnostics within their stated scope. Do not escalate from “inspect” to “apply”.\n\n## Separate claims\n\nKeep these claims independent:\n\n1. **source claim** — which repository/ref/revision defines desired state;\n2. **graph claim** — what the evaluated Alchemy stack intends;\n3. **state claim** — what Alchemy state records;\n4. **provider claim** — what the provider independently reports;\n5. **deployment claim** — which source artifact a runtime serves;\n6. **Preview claim** — behaviour in the Preview environment;\n7. **Production claim** — behaviour in Production;\n8. **public claim** — what an external caller actually observes.\n\nNever use one claim as shorthand for another. For example:\n\n- a green deployment job does not prove the public asset path;\n- a resource in state does not prove it still exists;\n- an HTTP 200 does not prove the expected redirect inventory;\n- an accepted telemetry request does not prove data is queryable;\n- a branch deployment does not prove default-branch establishment.\n\n## Bound custom configuration of foreign resources\n\nIf Alchemy configures a resource deployed elsewhere:\n\n- name the foreign deployment owner;\n- manage only a closed field set;\n- read the current value before writing;\n- compute a semantic diff;\n- reject unrelated changes;\n- use retain by default;\n- read back the exact field after write;\n- never claim deployment provenance.\n\nThis pattern suits narrowly governed environment variables, redirect URIs, or observability drains. It does not justify wrapping an entire provider client.\n# Cloudflare\n\n## Contents\n\n- [Ownership decisions](#ownership-decisions)\n- [File ownership](#file-ownership)\n- [Worker and asset semantics](#worker-and-asset-semantics)\n- [Routes zones and domains](#routes-zones-and-domains)\n- [Bindings and storage](#bindings-and-storage)\n- [Website origin rules](#website-origin-rules)\n- [Secrets](#secrets)\n- [Observability](#observability)\n- [State](#state)\n- [Readback checklist](#readback-checklist)\n\n## Ownership decisions\n\nDeclare which system owns each Cloudflare lifecycle:\n\n- Worker or static-asset deployment;\n- routes and custom domains;\n- DNS/zone configuration;\n- R2, D1, KV, Durable Objects, Queues, or Secrets Store;\n- environment secrets and bindings;\n- logs, metrics, traces, and observability destinations;\n- remote state infrastructure.\n\nDo not assume Alchemy owns all Cloudflare resources because it owns one Worker.\n\n## File ownership\n\nFor a small graph:\n\n```text\nsrc/lib/build/cloudflare.ts\n```\n\nFor a substantial graph:\n\n```text\nsrc/lib/build/cloudflare/\n  stack.ts\n  website.ts\n  worker.ts\n  storage.ts\n  routes.ts\n  secrets.ts\n  observability.ts\n```\n\nKeep stage/config Schemas outside provider declarations when other providers share them.\n\n## Worker and asset semantics\n\nMake asset routing policy explicit. In particular, distinguish asset-first and Worker-first behaviour and test the intended choice. A deployed Worker and green CI do not prove that static assets return the correct content type or bypass the Worker.\n\nTest:\n\n- root document;\n- hashed CSS and JavaScript assets;\n- missing assets;\n- API paths;\n- redirects;\n- cache headers;\n- content types;\n- route precedence.\n\nRecord compatibility date, compatibility flags, runtime limits, module format, and bundler inputs in a stable typed owner. Keep application and deployment configuration aligned through one contract or an invariant test.\n\n## Routes, zones, and domains\n\nTreat a zone or domain as foreign unless this repository creates and owns it.\n\n- Read the target zone before applying routes.\n- Adopt only under explicit authority.\n- Default adopted zones and routes to retain.\n- Restrict Production route adoption to the Production stage.\n- Reject a route whose account/zone does not match the authority record.\n- Read back the route pattern and bound Worker after apply.\n- Prove the public host independently.\n\nDo not infer route correctness from a Worker URL.\n\n## Bindings and storage\n\nModel each binding as typed desired state:\n\n- binding name;\n- resource kind;\n- physical resource identity;\n- stage/environment;\n- creation/adoption policy;\n- removal policy.\n\nAssert that application code and deployment configuration use the same binding names. For Durable Objects, include class name, migration/tag policy, and deployment sequencing. For R2/D1/KV, make data retention explicit and default destructive deletion to forbidden until authorised.\n\n## Website origin rules\n\nAn origin is the protocol and host, with an optional port, such as\n`http://127.0.0.1:3210`. The deployed host owns that address. Applications must\nnever hard-code it or build it from a stage, Worker name, account subdomain,\ncustom domain or development port.\n\nFor Alchemy `2.0.0-beta.79`, use the native self URL binding on the host that\nneeds its own address:\n\n```typescript\nconst website =\n  yield *\n  Cloudflare.Website.Vite(\"Website\", {\n    rootDir: \"apps/web\",\n    env: { PUBLIC_ORIGIN: Cloudflare.Worker.URL },\n  });\n\n// The same binding works in a native Worker declaration.\nconst api =\n  yield *\n  Cloudflare.Worker(\"Api\", {\n    main: \"./src/worker.ts\",\n    env: { PUBLIC_ORIGIN: Cloudflare.Worker.URL },\n  });\n```\n\nAlchemy resolves this binding to the local development proxy during local\ndevelopment, the deployed `workers.dev` address, or the configured custom\ndomain. Do not add separate local/cloud address formulas or a copied-address\nfallback. For Vite browser code, a `VITE_`-prefixed self URL binding uses the\nsame native resolution before the client bundle is built.\n\nIt is valid to declare a chosen custom domain as infrastructure input, for\nexample `domain: \"app.example.com\"`. Keep the application's own\n`PUBLIC_ORIGIN` bound to `Cloudflare.Worker.URL`; do not repeat that domain in\nits environment or build `\"https://\" + domain` there. Aliases and incoming\nrequest origins are separate from the host's configured canonical address.\n\nFor a different host, use that resource's native Output, such as `api.url`.\nPass the Output to the receiving resource; check its resolved value with the\nowning Schema when application code reads the binding. If the native address\ncan be absent, preserve that absence as `null` for an env binding and reject it\nwith the Schema. Do not replace an absent Output with a guessed address.\n\nKeep expected validation failures typed. Beta.79's `Output.mapEffect` callback\naccepts no expected errors, so do not force Schema decoding into it with\n`Effect.orDie`. `Output.map` may select a checked resource attribute or represent\nabsence; it must not reconstruct the hostname. Resolve and decode Outputs\nseparately before writing workflow or receipt values.\n\nCheck the selected Alchemy version's self URL, Vite, native Worker and local\nprovider APIs before copying this example. See the\n[matching primary sources](../sources.md#cloudflare-website-origins).\nLocal source/type checks do not prove live domain routing.\n\n## Separate Website and native API Workers\n\nUse standard Website.Vite for the frontend and a native Effect Worker for the\nAPI. The root stack deploys both with exact same-stage service/public bindings.\nPreview has its own API; a Website preview parent does not authorise using the\nProduction API. Do not wrap TanStack in a backend Worker or recreate an API\nruntime around the native incoming request fibre.\n\nCheck the installed Alchemy build/runtime mechanism before reading deployment\nservices in a Worker declaration. The qualified beta.79 runtime build guard\nremoves stack-only requirements from the uploaded entry. Test the real native\nentry without those services, return from startup, then request and release\nresources. A typecheck or successful upload alone does not prove startup.\n\nTest browser CORS with the native client's actual headers and an actual browser\ncall. Tracing headers b3 and traceparent are part of the qualified client's\nrequest; disabling tracing to avoid preflight is not a correction. Separate\nremote MCP Origin validation from matching-origin Website WebMCP registration.\n\n## Secrets\n\nPrefer provider-managed secret references or Secrets Store bindings over plaintext workflow values.\n\n- Acquire credentials through redacted Config.\n- Validate secret binding names and stage.\n- Never emit values in plans, logs, outputs, or receipts.\n- Separate credential issuance from resource apply.\n- Read back metadata or binding presence, never the secret.\n- Revoke disposable credentials after lifecycle proof.\n\n## Observability\n\nCloudflare platform observability and application telemetry are separate sources.\n\nFor Cloudflare-native logs or destinations:\n\n- identify Worker/service/environment/stage;\n- define retention and sampling;\n- read back the destination/binding;\n- query the downstream provider.\n\nFor Effect OTLP export:\n\n- keep endpoint/token in a redacted binding;\n- decode the binding at application ingress;\n- attach one exporter Layer to the runtime;\n- preserve `waitUntil` or the host completion mechanism;\n- test transport wiring;\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"export interface PlatformProps {\n  /**\n   * @internal type used to signal when this is an effect-native implementation\n   * @default false\n   */\n  isExternal?: boolean;\n}\n\n/**\n * Provide the platform class's layer (`cls.make(props, impl)`) with a\n * lifetime that matches the phase.\n *\n * **At runtime** (`__ALCHEMY_RUNTIME__`, folded to `true` in every bundled\n * artifact) the layer builds against the AMBIENT scope. `Effect.provide` is\n * implemented with `scopedWith`, so its transient region scope would tear\n * the layer down — firing init-level finalizers and releasing\n * `Layer.scoped` services — the moment init completes. The runtime bridges\n * evaluate the entrypoint under the instance-lifetime build scope (closed\n * at instance shutdown where the platform offers one — Lambda's SIGTERM\n * window — and never on workerd), so building against it keeps instance\n * services alive for the instance.\n *\n * **At plan/deploy** it stays `Effect.provide`: the transient region evicts\n * the layer's memo entry when each `yield*` of the class completes, so\n * every deploy in a session re-evaluates the resource and re-registers its\n * Output sources. Building on the session scope would keep the memo alive\n * across deploys and a second `stack.deploy` would skip source\n * registration (`MissingSourceError`).\n */\nconst provideClassLayer =\n  <ROut, E2, RIn>(layer: Layer.Layer<ROut, E2, RIn>) =>\n  <A, E, R>(\n    self: Effect.Effect<A, E, R>,\n  ): Effect.Effect<A, E | E2, RIn | Scope | Exclude<R, ROut>> =>\n    (globalThis.__ALCHEMY_RUNTIME__\n      ? Effect.flatMap(Effect.scope, (scope) =>\n          Effect.flatMap(Layer.buildWithScope(layer, scope), (context) =>\n            Effect.provideContext(self, context),\n          ),\n        )\n      : Effect.provide(self, layer)) as Effect.Effect<\n      A,\n      E | E2,\n      RIn | Scope | Exclude<R, ROut>\n    >;\n\nexport type Main<InitServices = never> = void | {\n  fetch?:\n    | HttpEffect<InitServices | PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope>\n    | Effect.Effect<\n        HttpEffect<InitServices | PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope>,\n        never,\n        InitServices | PlatformServices\n      >;\n};\n\nexport interface MainRpc<Req = never> {\n  [key: string]:\n    | Effect.Effect<\n        any,\n        any,\n        PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | HttpServerRequest | Scope | Req\n      >\n    | Stream.Stream<\n        any,\n        any,\n        PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | HttpServerRequest | Scope | Req\n      >\n    | ((\n        ...args: any[]\n      ) =>\n        | Effect.Effect<\n            any,\n            any,\n            PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope | Req\n          >\n        | Stream.Stream<\n            any,\n            any,\n            PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope | Req\n          >);\n}\n\n// Strip `void`/`undefined`/`never` from `Shape` before intersecting it with\n// `BaseShape`. This matters when `Shape` fails its `extends MainShape`\n// constraint (e.g. a `fetch` handler that leaks an error): TS clamps `Shape`\n// to the constraint union `void | { fetch: ... }`, and a *distributive*\n// conditional would split that into `BaseShape | ({ fetch } & BaseShape)` — a\n// union. Feeding a union into the `new (_: never): ...` construct signature\n// makes the base class a union type, which surfaces as the cryptic\n// ts(2509) \"Base constructor return type ... is not an object type\" instead of\n// the real assignability error on the `impl` argument. Excluding `void` here\n// keeps the construct-sig return a single object type, so only the actionable\n// error remains.\nexport type MakeShape<Shape, BaseShape> = [\n  Exclude<Shape, void | undefined>,\n] extends [never]\n  ? Exclude<BaseShape, void | undefined>\n  : Exclude<Shape, void | undefined> & Exclude<BaseShape, void | undefined>;\n\n// Services provided to the Resource's init/props effects. Deliberately does\n// NOT include `Scope`: init runs once per instance under a build scope that\n// closes at instance shutdown at best (Lambda's SIGTERM window; never on\n// workerd), so init code that needs a scope must not typecheck. Handlers get\n// a fresh per-event `Scope` from the bridge — note the explicit `| Scope` on\n// the handler positions in `Main` / `MainRpc` above.\nexport type PlatformServices =\n  | NodeServices\n  | HttpClient\n  | Provider<any>\n  | ProviderCollectionLike\n  | Stack\n  | StackServices\n  | Stage;\n\n/** A platform declaration's logical identity, readable without yielding it. */\nexport interface PlatformIdentity<Id extends string = string> {\n  readonly LogicalId: Id;\n}\n\n    createRuntimeContext: (id: string) => BaseRuntimeContext;\n    /**\n     * Legacy type names this platform's resource was previously registered\n     * under (see `ResourceOptions.aliases`) — threaded to the underlying\n     * `Resource` so state persisted under a pre-rename type keeps\n     * resolving.\n     */\n    aliases?: string[];\n    // `onCreate` runs inside the resource-construction context, which already\n    // carries the Stack's providers — so the hook may yield child resources\n    // (e.g. an async Worker registering a `WorkflowResource` for a bound\n    // Workflow). Allow an ambient requirement (`any`) rather than forcing\n    // `never`; it is discharged by the surrounding provider context.\n    onCreate?: (resource: R, props: any) => Effect.Effect<void, never, any>;\n    /**\n     * Transform the user-supplied props before the underlying resource is\n     * declared. Runs in the resource-construction context (Stack, Namespace,\n     * and providers are available), so the hook may compose REAL child\n     * resources (the `StaticSite` pattern) and return derived props that\n     * reference their Outputs. Implementations MUST be a no-op at runtime\n     * (guard on `globalThis.__ALCHEMY_RUNTIME__`) — the hook is evaluated\n     * wherever the props effect is, including inside deployed bundles.\n     */\n    transformProps?: (id: string, props: any) => Effect.Effect<any, any, any>;\n  },\n  methods?: { [key: string]: any },\n): any => {\n  type Props = any;\n  type Impl = Effect.Effect<any>;\n\n  // Platform registrations must have resolved props by plan time: every\n  // legitimate construction (a `.make(props, impl)` Layer build, a tag\n  // declared with props, a plain call) produces them, so props still\n  // `undefined` at plan can only be a bare-tag forward reference whose\n  // `.make` Layer was never provided — `Plan.make` fails fast naming the\n  // class and its Layer (#1054).\n  const resource = Resource(type, {\n    aliases: hooks.aliases,\n    requiresImplementation: true,\n  });\n  const PlatformContext = RuntimeContext;\n\n  // Apply the optional `transformProps` hook to a (possibly Effect-valued)\n  // props argument. Returns the props untouched when no hook is installed so\n  // the plain-object fast paths below keep working.\n  const applyTransformProps = (id: string, props: any): any =>\n    hooks.transformProps === undefined\n      ? props\n      : Effect.flatMap(\n          Effect.isEffect(props)\n            ? (props as Effect.Effect<any>)\n          Self,\n          Effect.flatMap(\n            Effect.all([\n              (() => {\n                const transformed = applyTransformProps(id, props);\n                return Effect.isEffect(transformed)\n                  ? (transformed as Effect.Effect<any>)\n                  : Effect.succeed(transformed ?? {});\n              })(),\n              Effect.sync(() => hooks.createRuntimeContext(id)),\n              Effect.context<never>(),\n            ]),\n            Effect.fn(function* ([props, runtimeContext, outerServices]) {\n              // The init effect (`impl`) is evaluated inside an\n              // `Effect.provide(...)` region below, whose implementation\n              // (`scopedWith`) would otherwise shadow the ambient `Scope`\n              // with a transient one that closes the moment init returns.\n              // Pin init's ambient scope to this layer's build scope\n              // instead: under the runtime bridges that scope belongs to\n              // the instance-lifetime build, so init-level finalizers run\n              // at instance shutdown or not at all — never per event\n              // (workerd never closes it; the Lambda entry closes it in the\n              // SIGTERM window). Request-coupled cleanup belongs in\n              // handlers, where the bridge provides a per-event scope.\n              const buildScope = yield* Effect.scope;\n              const instance = Object.assign(\n                yield* resource(id, props as any).pipe(\n                  Effect.flatMap(\n                    (resource) =>\n                      hooks\n                        .onCreate?.(resource, props)\n                        .pipe(Effect.map(() => resource)) ??\n                      Effect.succeed(resource),\n                  ),\n                ),\n                runtimeContext,\n              );\n\n              yield* impl.pipe(\n                Effect.flatMap((impl) => {\n                  if (!impl) return Effect.void;\n                  const shape = impl as Record<string, unknown>;\n                  // Serve when there's a `fetch` handler OR any RPC shape\n                  // methods. A pure-RPC impl (methods, no `fetch`) still needs\n                  // the server to boot — hand `serveRpc` a default 404 fallback\n                  // so `/__rpc__/*` is dispatched to the shape methods and\n                  // everything else 404s.\n                  // May be an `HttpEffect` or an Effect resolving to one (the\n                  // `Main.fetch` shape); `serve` accepts both.\n                  const fetch = shape.fetch as any;\n                  const hasRpcMethods = Object.keys(shape).some(\n                    (key) => key !== \"fetch\",\n                  );\n                  if (!fetch && !hasRpcMethods) return Effect.void;\n                  // Hand the full impl to `serve` so the runtime can expose any\n                  // non-handler methods on the impl shape (RPC methods)\n * **Example:** Enabling Workers Cache on an async Worker\n * ```typescript\n * {\n *   main: \"./src/worker.ts\",\n *   cache: {\n *     enabled: true,\n *     crossVersionCache: true,\n *   },\n * }\n * ```\n *\n * ### Background Work & Scopes\n * Each incoming event (fetch, RPC call, scheduled run) gets its own Effect\n * `Scope`. When the handler finishes, the bridge closes that scope and\n * registers the close promise with workerd's `ctx.waitUntil` — so\n * finalizers added with `Effect.addFinalizer` inside a handler run *after*\n * the response is sent, without blocking it, and the Worker stays alive\n * until they settle. Streaming responses transfer the scope to the stream,\n * so those finalizers run when the stream completes instead.\n *\n * For ad-hoc background work, `WorkerExecutionContext.waitUntil(effect)`\n * forks an Effect with the caller's full context and keeps the invocation\n * alive until it settles. The context can be yielded once in the\n * constructor and used from any handler; its methods are `RuntimeContext`-\n * colored, so they can only run inside a handler.\n *\n * The constructor is evaluated once per isolate: the bridge builds the\n * Worker's layer stack on the first event and every later event reuses the\n * built services. Resolve services, bind resources, build handlers there —\n * one-shot I/O that caches a plain value (e.g. fetching a secret for a\n * client) is fine, but nothing disposable: the build scope is never closed\n * (workerd has no isolate-teardown hook), so a finalizer added in the\n * constructor never runs, and I/O-backed objects (sockets, response bodies) are\n * pinned to the request that created them. Anything that needs cleanup\n * belongs in a handler, where `Effect.addFinalizer` attaches to the\n * per-event scope.\n *\n * **Example:** Post-response cleanup with a scope finalizer\n * ```typescript\n * return {\n *   fetch: Effect.gen(function* () {\n *     // runs after this response is sent, kept alive by waitUntil\n *     yield* Effect.addFinalizer(() => flushMetrics().pipe(Effect.ignore));\n *     return HttpServerResponse.text(\"ok\");\n *   }),\n * };\n * ```\n# Ownership and Boundaries\n\n## Contents\n\n- [Start with lifecycle ownership](#start-with-lifecycle-ownership)\n- [Write an ownership matrix](#write-an-ownership-matrix)\n- [Decide when Alchemy should own a resource](#decide-when-alchemy-should-own-a-resource)\n- [Separate authority from capability](#separate-authority-from-capability)\n- [Separate claims](#separate-claims)\n- [Bound custom configuration of foreign resources](#bound-custom-configuration-of-foreign-resources)\n\n## Start with lifecycle ownership\n\nDo not begin with provider APIs. Begin with a resource inventory and name the system that owns each lifecycle transition.\n\nUse these lifecycle verbs precisely:\n\n- **declare**: describe desired state without claiming mutation;\n- **create**: make a resource that does not exist;\n- **adopt**: attach state to a pre-existing resource;\n- **observe**: read and report without changing;\n- **configure**: mutate a bounded setting on a resource owned elsewhere;\n- **reconcile**: converge the managed fields to desired state;\n- **deploy**: create a runtime or artifact from a source revision;\n- **retain**: remove the resource from this stack without deleting it;\n- **destroy**: delete the provider resource and verify absence.\n\nDo not collapse these verbs into “manage”.\n\n## Write an ownership matrix\n\nBefore implementation, record:\n\n| Resource            | Lifecycle owner                   | Deployment owner | State owner   | Readback authority | Removal policy |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------- | ------------------ | -------------- |\n| Cloudflare Worker   | Alchemy or Vercel Git, choose one | named workflow   | named backend | Cloudflare API     | delete/retain  |\n| Vercel deployment   | Vercel Git by default             | Vercel Git       | Vercel        | Vercel API         | Vercel policy  |\n| Axiom dataset       | Alchemy                           | not applicable   | Alchemy state | Axiom API/query    | retain/delete  |\n| WorkOS redirect URI | WorkOS or narrow custom resource  | not applicable   | explicit      | WorkOS API         | delete/retain  |\n\nAdd account, organisation, project, tenant, zone, or team identifiers when ambiguity is possible. Store safe identifiers, never credentials.\n\n## Decide when Alchemy should own a resource\n\nUse Alchemy when all relevant conditions hold:\n\n- the repository owns the desired state;\n- the lifecycle can be expressed deterministically;\n- independent readback exists;\n- create/update/delete semantics are understood;\n- retries can converge;\n- partial failure can be recovered;\n- stage identity can prevent collisions;\n- credentials can be scoped to the authorised operation;\n- the repository can test the graph and the provider result.\n\nPrefer another owner or no new IaC when:\n\n- Vercel Git already owns deployments and source provenance;\n- a resource is manually governed for a documented reason;\n- the provider API cannot support safe read, update, or delete semantics;\n- the only available credential is materially over-broad;\n- the repository cannot distinguish its resource from a foreign one;\n- live state cannot be read independently;\n- the proposed module exists only to make the tree look symmetrical.\n\nAn explicit “no Alchemy role” architecture decision is stronger than an unused scaffold.\n\n## Separate authority from capability\n\nA credential that can mutate a provider does not authorise every mutation it permits.\n\nRequire an operation-specific authority record:\n\n- operation: plan, create, update, adopt, reconcile, destroy, issue token;\n- stage and environment;\n- exact resource set;\n- expected source revision;\n- credential provenance;\n- allowed replacement/deletion;\n- expiry or single-use constraint where appropriate;\n- required preflight and readback;\n- rollback or recovery owner.\n\nKeep provider discovery and read-only diagnostics within their stated scope. Do not escalate from “inspect” to “apply”.\n\n## Separate claims\n\nKeep these claims independent:\n\n1. **source claim** — which repository/ref/revision defines desired state;\n2. **graph claim** — what the evaluated Alchemy stack intends;\n3. **state claim** — what Alchemy state records;\n4. **provider claim** — what the provider independently reports;\n5. **deployment claim** — which source artifact a runtime serves;\n6. **Preview claim** — behaviour in the Preview environment;\n7. **Production claim** — behaviour in Production;\n8. **public claim** — what an external caller actually observes.\n\nNever use one claim as shorthand for another. For example:\n\n- a green deployment job does not prove the public asset path;\n- a resource in state does not prove it still exists;\n- an HTTP 200 does not prove the expected redirect inventory;\n- an accepted telemetry request does not prove data is queryable;\n- a branch deployment does not prove default-branch establishment.\n\n## Bound custom configuration of foreign resources\n\nIf Alchemy configures a resource deployed elsewhere:\n\n- name the foreign deployment owner;\n- manage only a closed field set;\n- read the current value before writing;\n- compute a semantic diff;\n- reject unrelated changes;\n- use retain by default;\n- read back the exact field after write;\n- never claim deployment provenance.\n\nThis pattern suits narrowly governed environment variables, redirect URIs, or observability drains. It does not justify wrapping an entire provider client.\n# Cloudflare\n\n## Contents\n\n- [Ownership decisions](#ownership-decisions)\n- [File ownership](#file-ownership)\n- [Worker and asset semantics](#worker-and-asset-semantics)\n- [Routes zones and domains](#routes-zones-and-domains)\n- [Bindings and storage](#bindings-and-storage)\n- [Website origin rules](#website-origin-rules)\n- [Secrets](#secrets)\n- [Observability](#observability)\n- [State](#state)\n- [Readback checklist](#readback-checklist)\n\n## Ownership decisions\n\nDeclare which system owns each Cloudflare lifecycle:\n\n- Worker or static-asset deployment;\n- routes and custom domains;\n- DNS/zone configuration;\n- R2, D1, KV, Durable Objects, Queues, or Secrets Store;\n- environment secrets and bindings;\n- logs, metrics, traces, and observability destinations;\n- remote state infrastructure.\n\nDo not assume Alchemy owns all Cloudflare resources because it owns one Worker.\n\n## File ownership\n\nFor a small graph:\n\n```text\nsrc/lib/build/cloudflare.ts\n```\n\nFor a substantial graph:\n\n```text\nsrc/lib/build/cloudflare/\n  stack.ts\n  website.ts\n  worker.ts\n  storage.ts\n  routes.ts\n  secrets.ts\n  observability.ts\n```\n\nKeep stage/config Schemas outside provider declarations when other providers share them.\n\n## Worker and asset semantics\n\nMake asset routing policy explicit. In particular, distinguish asset-first and Worker-first behaviour and test the intended choice. A deployed Worker and green CI do not prove that static assets return the correct content type or bypass the Worker.\n\nTest:\n\n- root document;\n- hashed CSS and JavaScript assets;\n- missing assets;\n- API paths;\n- redirects;\n- cache headers;\n- content types;\n- route precedence.\n\nRecord compatibility date, compatibility flags, runtime limits, module format, and bundler inputs in a stable typed owner. Keep application and deployment configuration aligned through one contract or an invariant test.\n\n## Routes, zones, and domains\n\nTreat a zone or domain as foreign unless this repository creates and owns it.\n\n- Read the target zone before applying routes.\n- Adopt only under explicit authority.\n- Default adopted zones and routes to retain.\n- Restrict Production route adoption to the Production stage.\n- Reject a route whose account/zone does not match the authority record.\n- Read back the route pattern and bound Worker after apply.\n- Prove the public host independently.\n\nDo not infer route correctness from a Worker URL.\n\n## Bindings and storage\n\nModel each binding as typed desired state:\n\n- binding name;\n- resource kind;\n- physical resource identity;\n- stage/environment;\n- creation/adoption policy;\n- removal policy.\n\nAssert that application code and deployment configuration use the same binding names. For Durable Objects, include class name, migration/tag policy, and deployment sequencing. For R2/D1/KV, make data retention explicit and default destructive deletion to forbidden until authorised.\n\n## Website origin rules\n\nAn origin is the protocol and host, with an optional port, such as\n`http://127.0.0.1:3210`. The deployed host owns that address. Applications must\nnever hard-code it or build it from a stage, Worker name, account subdomain,\ncustom domain or development port.\n\nFor Alchemy `2.0.0-beta.79`, use the native self URL binding on the host that\nneeds its own address:\n\n```typescript\nconst website =\n  yield *\n  Cloudflare.Website.Vite(\"Website\", {\n    rootDir: \"apps/web\",\n    env: { PUBLIC_ORIGIN: Cloudflare.Worker.URL },\n  });\n\n// The same binding works in a native Worker declaration.\nconst api =\n  yield *\n  Cloudflare.Worker(\"Api\", {\n    main: \"./src/worker.ts\",\n    env: { PUBLIC_ORIGIN: Cloudflare.Worker.URL },\n  });\n```\n\nAlchemy resolves this binding to the local development proxy during local\ndevelopment, the deployed `workers.dev` address, or the configured custom\ndomain. Do not add separate local/cloud address formulas or a copied-address\nfallback. For Vite browser code, a `VITE_`-prefixed self URL binding uses the\nsame native resolution before the client bundle is built.\n\nIt is valid to declare a chosen custom domain as infrastructure input, for\nexample `domain: \"app.example.com\"`. Keep the application's own\n`PUBLIC_ORIGIN` bound to `Cloudflare.Worker.URL`; do not repeat that domain in\nits environment or build `\"https://\" + domain` there. Aliases and incoming\nrequest origins are separate from the host's configured canonical address.\n\nFor a different host, use that resource's native Output, such as `api.url`.\nPass the Output to the receiving resource; check its resolved value with the\nowning Schema when application code reads the binding. If the native address\ncan be absent, preserve that absence as `null` for an env binding and reject it\nwith the Schema. Do not replace an absent Output with a guessed address.\n\nKeep expected validation failures typed. Beta.79's `Output.mapEffect` callback\naccepts no expected errors, so do not force Schema decoding into it with\n`Effect.orDie`. `Output.map` may select a checked resource attribute or represent\nabsence; it must not reconstruct the hostname. Resolve and decode Outputs\nseparately before writing workflow or receipt values.\n\nCheck the selected Alchemy version's self URL, Vite, native Worker and local\nprovider APIs before copying this example. See the\n[matching primary sources](../sources.md#cloudflare-website-origins).\nLocal source/type checks do not prove live domain routing.\n\n## Separate Website and native API Workers\n\nUse standard Website.Vite for the frontend and a native Effect Worker for the\nAPI. The root stack deploys both with exact same-stage service/public bindings.\nPreview has its own API; a Website preview parent does not authorise using the\nProduction API. Do not wrap TanStack in a backend Worker or recreate an API\nruntime around the native incoming request fibre.\n\nCheck the installed Alchemy build/runtime mechanism before reading deployment\nservices in a Worker declaration. The qualified beta.79 runtime build guard\nremoves stack-only requirements from the uploaded entry. Test the real native\nentry without those services, return from startup, then request and release\nresources. A typecheck or successful upload alone does not prove startup.\n\nTest browser CORS with the native client's actual headers and an actual browser\ncall. Tracing headers b3 and traceparent are part of the qualified client's\nrequest; disabling tracing to avoid preflight is not a correction. Separate\nremote MCP Origin validation from matching-origin Website WebMCP registration.\n\n## Secrets\n\nPrefer provider-managed secret references or Secrets Store bindings over plaintext workflow values.\n\n- Acquire credentials through redacted Config.\n- Validate secret binding names and stage.\n- Never emit values in plans, logs, outputs, or receipts.\n- Separate credential issuance from resource apply.\n- Read back metadata or binding presence, never the secret.\n- Revoke disposable credentials after lifecycle proof.\n\n## Observability\n\nCloudflare platform observability and application telemetry are separate sources.\n\nFor Cloudflare-native logs or destinations:\n\n- identify Worker/service/environment/stage;\n- define retention and sampling;\n- read back the destination/binding;\n- query the downstream provider.\n\nFor Effect OTLP export:\n\n- keep endpoint/token in a redacted binding;\n- decode the binding at application ingress;\n- attach one exporter Layer to the runtime;\n- preserve `waitUntil` or the host completion mechanism;\n- test transport wiring;\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":5792},"formatted_output":"export interface PlatformProps {\n  /**\n   * @internal type used to signal when this is an effect-native implementation\n   * @default false\n   */\n  isExternal?: boolean;\n}\n\n/**\n * Provide the platform class's layer (`cls.make(props, impl)`) with a\n * lifetime that matches the phase.\n *\n * **At runtime** (`__ALCHEMY_RUNTIME__`, folded to `true` in every bundled\n * artifact) the layer builds against the AMBIENT scope. `Effect.provide` is\n * implemented with `scopedWith`, so its transient region scope would tear\n * the layer down — firing init-level finalizers and releasing\n * `Layer.scoped` services — the moment init completes. The runtime bridges\n * evaluate the entrypoint under the instance-lifetime build scope (closed\n * at instance shutdown where the platform offers one — Lambda's SIGTERM\n * window — and never on workerd), so building against it keeps instance\n * services alive for the instance.\n *\n * **At plan/deploy** it stays `Effect.provide`: the transient region evicts\n * the layer's memo entry when each `yield*` of the class completes, so\n * every deploy in a session re-evaluates the resource and re-registers its\n * Output sources. Building on the session scope would keep the memo alive\n * across deploys and a second `stack.deploy` would skip source\n * registration (`MissingSourceError`).\n */\nconst provideClassLayer =\n  <ROut, E2, RIn>(layer: Layer.Layer<ROut, E2, RIn>) =>\n  <A, E, R>(\n    self: Effect.Effect<A, E, R>,\n  ): Effect.Effect<A, E | E2, RIn | Scope | Exclude<R, ROut>> =>\n    (globalThis.__ALCHEMY_RUNTIME__\n      ? Effect.flatMap(Effect.scope, (scope) =>\n          Effect.flatMap(Layer.buildWithScope(layer, scope), (context) =>\n            Effect.provideContext(self, context),\n          ),\n        )\n      : Effect.provide(self, layer)) as Effect.Effect<\n      A,\n      E | E2,\n      RIn | Scope | Exclude<R, ROut>\n    >;\n\nexport type Main<InitServices = never> = void | {\n  fetch?:\n    | HttpEffect<InitServices | PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope>\n    | Effect.Effect<\n        HttpEffect<InitServices | PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope>,\n        never,\n        InitServices | PlatformServices\n      >;\n};\n\nexport interface MainRpc<Req = never> {\n  [key: string]:\n    | Effect.Effect<\n        any,\n        any,\n        PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | HttpServerRequest | Scope | Req\n      >\n    | Stream.Stream<\n        any,\n        any,\n        PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | HttpServerRequest | Scope | Req\n      >\n    | ((\n        ...args: any[]\n      ) =>\n        | Effect.Effect<\n            any,\n            any,\n            PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope | Req\n          >\n        | Stream.Stream<\n            any,\n            any,\n            PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope | Req\n          >);\n}\n\n// Strip `void`/`undefined`/`never` from `Shape` before intersecting it with\n// `BaseShape`. This matters when `Shape` fails its `extends MainShape`\n// constraint (e.g. a `fetch` handler that leaks an error): TS clamps `Shape`\n// to the constraint union `void | { fetch: ... }`, and a *distributive*\n// conditional would split that into `BaseShape | ({ fetch } & BaseShape)` — a\n// union. Feeding a union into the `new (_: never): ...` construct signature\n// makes the base class a union type, which surfaces as the cryptic\n// ts(2509) \"Base constructor return type ... is not an object type\" instead of\n// the real assignability error on the `impl` argument. Excluding `void` here\n// keeps the construct-sig return a single object type, so only the actionable\n// error remains.\nexport type MakeShape<Shape, BaseShape> = [\n  Exclude<Shape, void | undefined>,\n] extends [never]\n  ? Exclude<BaseShape, void | undefined>\n  : Exclude<Shape, void | undefined> & Exclude<BaseShape, void | undefined>;\n\n// Services provided to the Resource's init/props effects. Deliberately does\n// NOT include `Scope`: init runs once per instance under a build scope that\n// closes at instance shutdown at best (Lambda's SIGTERM window; never on\n// workerd), so init code that needs a scope must not typecheck. Handlers get\n// a fresh per-event `Scope` from the bridge — note the explicit `| Scope` on\n// the handler positions in `Main` / `MainRpc` above.\nexport type PlatformServices =\n  | NodeServices\n  | HttpClient\n  | Provider<any>\n  | ProviderCollectionLike\n  | Stack\n  | StackServices\n  | Stage;\n\n/** A platform declaration's logical identity, readable without yielding it. */\nexport interface PlatformIdentity<Id extends string = string> {\n  readonly LogicalId: Id;\n}\n\n    createRuntimeContext: (id: string) => BaseRuntimeContext;\n    /**\n     * Legacy type names this platform's resource was previously registered\n     * under (see `ResourceOptions.aliases`) — threaded to the underlying\n     * `Resource` so state persisted under a pre-rename type keeps\n     * resolving.\n     */\n    aliases?: string[];\n    // `onCreate` runs inside the resource-construction context, which already\n    // carries the Stack's providers — so the hook may yield child resources\n    // (e.g. an async Worker registering a `WorkflowResource` for a bound\n    // Workflow). Allow an ambient requirement (`any`) rather than forcing\n    // `never`; it is discharged by the surrounding provider context.\n    onCreate?: (resource: R, props: any) => Effect.Effect<void, never, any>;\n    /**\n     * Transform the user-supplied props before the underlying resource is\n     * declared. Runs in the resource-construction context (Stack, Namespace,\n     * and providers are available), so the hook may compose REAL child\n     * resources (the `StaticSite` pattern) and return derived props that\n     * reference their Outputs. Implementations MUST be a no-op at runtime\n     * (guard on `globalThis.__ALCHEMY_RUNTIME__`) — the hook is evaluated\n     * wherever the props effect is, including inside deployed bundles.\n     */\n    transformProps?: (id: string, props: any) => Effect.Effect<any, any, any>;\n  },\n  methods?: { [key: string]: any },\n): any => {\n  type Props = any;\n  type Impl = Effect.Effect<any>;\n\n  // Platform registrations must have resolved props by plan time: every\n  // legitimate construction (a `.make(props, impl)` Layer build, a tag\n  // declared with props, a plain call) produces them, so props still\n  // `undefined` at plan can only be a bare-tag forward reference whose\n  // `.make` Layer was never provided — `Plan.make` fails fast naming the\n  // class and its Layer (#1054).\n  const resource = Resource(type, {\n    aliases: hooks.aliases,\n    requiresImplementation: true,\n  });\n  const PlatformContext = RuntimeContext;\n\n  // Apply the optional `transformProps` hook to a (possibly Effect-valued)\n  // props argument. Returns the props untouched when no hook is installed so\n  // the plain-object fast paths below keep working.\n  const applyTransformProps = (id: string, props: any): any =>\n    hooks.transformProps === undefined\n      ? props\n      : Effect.flatMap(\n          Effect.isEffect(props)\n            ? (props as Effect.Effect<any>)\n          Self,\n          Effect.flatMap(\n            Effect.all([\n              (() => {\n                const transformed = applyTransformProps(id, props);\n                return Effect.isEffect(transformed)\n                  ? (transformed as Effect.Effect<any>)\n                  : Effect.succeed(transformed ?? {});\n              })(),\n              Effect.sync(() => hooks.createRuntimeContext(id)),\n              Effect.context<never>(),\n            ]),\n            Effect.fn(function* ([props, runtimeContext, outerServices]) {\n              // The init effect (`impl`) is evaluated inside an\n              // `Effect.provide(...)` region below, whose implementation\n              // (`scopedWith`) would otherwise shadow the ambient `Scope`\n              // with a transient one that closes the moment init returns.\n              // Pin init's ambient scope to this layer's build scope\n              // instead: under the runtime bridges that scope belongs to\n              // the instance-lifetime build, so init-level finalizers run\n              // at instance shutdown or not at all — never per event\n              // (workerd never closes it; the Lambda entry closes it in the\n              // SIGTERM window). Request-coupled cleanup belongs in\n              // handlers, where the bridge provides a per-event scope.\n              const buildScope = yield* Effect.scope;\n              const instance = Object.assign(\n                yield* resource(id, props as any).pipe(\n                  Effect.flatMap(\n                    (resource) =>\n                      hooks\n                        .onCreate?.(resource, props)\n                        .pipe(Effect.map(() => resource)) ??\n                      Effect.succeed(resource),\n                  ),\n                ),\n                runtimeContext,\n              );\n\n              yield* impl.pipe(\n                Effect.flatMap((impl) => {\n                  if (!impl) return Effect.void;\n                  const shape = impl as Record<string, unknown>;\n                  // Serve when there's a `fetch` handler OR any RPC shape\n                  // methods. A pure-RPC impl (methods, no `fetch`) still needs\n                  // the server to boot — hand `serveRpc` a default 404 fallback\n                  // so `/__rpc__/*` is dispatched to the shape methods and\n                  // everything else 404s.\n                  // May be an `HttpEffect` or an Effect resolving to one (the\n                  // `Main.fetch` shape); `serve` accepts both.\n                  const fetch = shape.fetch as any;\n                  const hasRpcMethods = Object.keys(shape).some(\n                    (key) => key !== \"fetch\",\n                  );\n                  if (!fetch && !hasRpcMethods) return Effect.void;\n                  // Hand the full impl to `serve` so the runtime can expose any\n                  // non-handler methods on the impl shape (RPC methods)\n * **Example:** Enabling Workers Cache on an async Worker\n * ```typescript\n * {\n *   main: \"./src/worker.ts\",\n *   cache: {\n *     enabled: true,\n *     crossVersionCache: true,\n *   },\n * }\n * ```\n *\n * ### Background Work & Scopes\n * Each incoming event (fetch, RPC call, scheduled run) gets its own Effect\n * `Scope`. When the handler finishes, the bridge closes that scope and\n * registers the close promise with workerd's `ctx.waitUntil` — so\n * finalizers added with `Effect.addFinalizer` inside a handler run *after*\n * the response is sent, without blocking it, and the Worker stays alive\n * until they settle. Streaming responses transfer the scope to the stream,\n * so those finalizers run when the stream completes instead.\n *\n * For ad-hoc background work, `WorkerExecutionContext.waitUntil(effect)`\n * forks an Effect with the caller's full context and keeps the invocation\n * alive until it settles. The context can be yielded once in the\n * constructor and used from any handler; its methods are `RuntimeContext`-\n * colored, so they can only run inside a handler.\n *\n * The constructor is evaluated once per isolate: the bridge builds the\n * Worker's layer stack on the first event and every later event reuses the\n * built services. Resolve services, bind resources, build handlers there —\n * one-shot I/O that caches a plain value (e.g. fetching a secret for a\n * client) is fine, but nothing disposable: the build scope is never closed\n * (workerd has no isolate-teardown hook), so a finalizer added in the\n * constructor never runs, and I/O-backed objects (sockets, response bodies) are\n * pinned to the request that created them. Anything that needs cleanup\n * belongs in a handler, where `Effect.addFinalizer` attaches to the\n * per-event scope.\n *\n * **Example:** Post-response cleanup with a scope finalizer\n * ```typescript\n * return {\n *   fetch: Effect.gen(function* () {\n *     // runs after this response is sent, kept alive by waitUntil\n *     yield* Effect.addFinalizer(() => flushMetrics().pipe(Effect.ignore));\n *     return HttpServerResponse.text(\"ok\");\n *   }),\n * };\n * ```\n# Ownership and Boundaries\n\n## Contents\n\n- [Start with lifecycle ownership](#start-with-lifecycle-ownership)\n- [Write an ownership matrix](#write-an-ownership-matrix)\n- [Decide when Alchemy should own a resource](#decide-when-alchemy-should-own-a-resource)\n- [Separate authority from capability](#separate-authority-from-capability)\n- [Separate claims](#separate-claims)\n- [Bound custom configuration of foreign resources](#bound-custom-configuration-of-foreign-resources)\n\n## Start with lifecycle ownership\n\nDo not begin with provider APIs. Begin with a resource inventory and name the system that owns each lifecycle transition.\n\nUse these lifecycle verbs precisely:\n\n- **declare**: describe desired state without claiming mutation;\n- **create**: make a resource that does not exist;\n- **adopt**: attach state to a pre-existing resource;\n- **observe**: read and report without changing;\n- **configure**: mutate a bounded setting on a resource owned elsewhere;\n- **reconcile**: converge the managed fields to desired state;\n- **deploy**: create a runtime or artifact from a source revision;\n- **retain**: remove the resource from this stack without deleting it;\n- **destroy**: delete the provider resource and verify absence.\n\nDo not collapse these verbs into “manage”.\n\n## Write an ownership matrix\n\nBefore implementation, record:\n\n| Resource            | Lifecycle owner                   | Deployment owner | State owner   | Readback authority | Removal policy |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------- | ------------------ | -------------- |\n| Cloudflare Worker   | Alchemy or Vercel Git, choose one | named workflow   | named backend | Cloudflare API     | delete/retain  |\n| Vercel deployment   | Vercel Git by default             | Vercel Git       | Vercel        | Vercel API         | Vercel policy  |\n| Axiom dataset       | Alchemy                           | not applicable   | Alchemy state | Axiom API/query    | retain/delete  |\n| WorkOS redirect URI | WorkOS or narrow custom resource  | not applicable   | explicit      | WorkOS API         | delete/retain  |\n\nAdd account, organisation, project, tenant, zone, or team identifiers when ambiguity is possible. Store safe identifiers, never credentials.\n\n## Decide when Alchemy should own a resource\n\nUse Alchemy when all relevant conditions hold:\n\n- the repository owns the desired state;\n- the lifecycle can be expressed deterministically;\n- independent readback exists;\n- create/update/delete semantics are understood;\n- retries can converge;\n- partial failure can be recovered;\n- stage identity can prevent collisions;\n- credentials can be scoped to the authorised operation;\n- the repository can test the graph and the provider result.\n\nPrefer another owner or no new IaC when:\n\n- Vercel Git already owns deployments and source provenance;\n- a resource is manually governed for a documented reason;\n- the provider API cannot support safe read, update, or delete semantics;\n- the only available credential is materially over-broad;\n- the repository cannot distinguish its resource from a foreign one;\n- live state cannot be read independently;\n- the proposed module exists only to make the tree look symmetrical.\n\nAn explicit “no Alchemy role” architecture decision is stronger than an unused scaffold.\n\n## Separate authority from capability\n\nA credential that can mutate a provider does not authorise every mutation it permits.\n\nRequire an operation-specific authority record:\n\n- operation: plan, create, update, adopt, reconcile, destroy, issue token;\n- stage and environment;\n- exact resource set;\n- expected source revision;\n- credential provenance;\n- allowed replacement/deletion;\n- expiry or single-use constraint where appropriate;\n- required preflight and readback;\n- rollback or recovery owner.\n\nKeep provider discovery and read-only diagnostics within their stated scope. Do not escalate from “inspect” to “apply”.\n\n## Separate claims\n\nKeep these claims independent:\n\n1. **source claim** — which repository/ref/revision defines desired state;\n2. **graph claim** — what the evaluated Alchemy stack intends;\n3. **state claim** — what Alchemy state records;\n4. **provider claim** — what the provider independently reports;\n5. **deployment claim** — which source artifact a runtime serves;\n6. **Preview claim** — behaviour in the Preview environment;\n7. **Production claim** — behaviour in Production;\n8. **public claim** — what an external caller actually observes.\n\nNever use one claim as shorthand for another. For example:\n\n- a green deployment job does not prove the public asset path;\n- a resource in state does not prove it still exists;\n- an HTTP 200 does not prove the expected redirect inventory;\n- an accepted telemetry request does not prove data is queryable;\n- a branch deployment does not prove default-branch establishment.\n\n## Bound custom configuration of foreign resources\n\nIf Alchemy configures a resource deployed elsewhere:\n\n- name the foreign deployment owner;\n- manage only a closed field set;\n- read the current value before writing;\n- compute a semantic diff;\n- reject unrelated changes;\n- use retain by default;\n- read back the exact field after write;\n- never claim deployment provenance.\n\nThis pattern suits narrowly governed environment variables, redirect URIs, or observability drains. It does not justify wrapping an entire provider client.\n# Cloudflare\n\n## Contents\n\n- [Ownership decisions](#ownership-decisions)\n- [File ownership](#file-ownership)\n- [Worker and asset semantics](#worker-and-asset-semantics)\n- [Routes zones and domains](#routes-zones-and-domains)\n- [Bindings and storage](#bindings-and-storage)\n- [Website origin rules](#website-origin-rules)\n- [Secrets](#secrets)\n- [Observability](#observability)\n- [State](#state)\n- [Readback checklist](#readback-checklist)\n\n## Ownership decisions\n\nDeclare which system owns each Cloudflare lifecycle:\n\n- Worker or static-asset deployment;\n- routes and custom domains;\n- DNS/zone configuration;\n- R2, D1, KV, Durable Objects, Queues, or Secrets Store;\n- environment secrets and bindings;\n- logs, metrics, traces, and observability destinations;\n- remote state infrastructure.\n\nDo not assume Alchemy owns all Cloudflare resources because it owns one Worker.\n\n## File ownership\n\nFor a small graph:\n\n```text\nsrc/lib/build/cloudflare.ts\n```\n\nFor a substantial graph:\n\n```text\nsrc/lib/build/cloudflare/\n  stack.ts\n  website.ts\n  worker.ts\n  storage.ts\n  routes.ts\n  secrets.ts\n  observability.ts\n```\n\nKeep stage/config Schemas outside provider declarations when other providers share them.\n\n## Worker and asset semantics\n\nMake asset routing policy explicit. In particular, distinguish asset-first and Worker-first behaviour and test the intended choice. A deployed Worker and green CI do not prove that static assets return the correct content type or bypass the Worker.\n\nTest:\n\n- root document;\n- hashed CSS and JavaScript assets;\n- missing assets;\n- API paths;\n- redirects;\n- cache headers;\n- content types;\n- route precedence.\n\nRecord compatibility date, compatibility flags, runtime limits, module format, and bundler inputs in a stable typed owner. Keep application and deployment configuration aligned through one contract or an invariant test.\n\n## Routes, zones, and domains\n\nTreat a zone or domain as foreign unless this repository creates and owns it.\n\n- Read the target zone before applying routes.\n- Adopt only under explicit authority.\n- Default adopted zones and routes to retain.\n- Restrict Production route adoption to the Production stage.\n- Reject a route whose account/zone does not match the authority record.\n- Read back the route pattern and bound Worker after apply.\n- Prove the public host independently.\n\nDo not infer route correctness from a Worker URL.\n\n## Bindings and storage\n\nModel each binding as typed desired state:\n\n- binding name;\n- resource kind;\n- physical resource identity;\n- stage/environment;\n- creation/adoption policy;\n- removal policy.\n\nAssert that application code and deployment configuration use the same binding names. For Durable Objects, include class name, migration/tag policy, and deployment sequencing. For R2/D1/KV, make data retention explicit and default destructive deletion to forbidden until authorised.\n\n## Website origin rules\n\nAn origin is the protocol and host, with an optional port, such as\n`http://127.0.0.1:3210`. The deployed host owns that address. Applications must\nnever hard-code it or build it from a stage, Worker name, account subdomain,\ncustom domain or development port.\n\nFor Alchemy `2.0.0-beta.79`, use the native self URL binding on the host that\nneeds its own address:\n\n```typescript\nconst website =\n  yield *\n  Cloudflare.Website.Vite(\"Website\", {\n    rootDir: \"apps/web\",\n    env: { PUBLIC_ORIGIN: Cloudflare.Worker.URL },\n  });\n\n// The same binding works in a native Worker declaration.\nconst api =\n  yield *\n  Cloudflare.Worker(\"Api\", {\n    main: \"./src/worker.ts\",\n    env: { PUBLIC_ORIGIN: Cloudflare.Worker.URL },\n  });\n```\n\nAlchemy resolves this binding to the local development proxy during local\ndevelopment, the deployed `workers.dev` address, or the configured custom\ndomain. Do not add separate local/cloud address formulas or a copied-address\nfallback. For Vite browser code, a `VITE_`-prefixed self URL binding uses the\nsame native resolution before the client bundle is built.\n\nIt is valid to declare a chosen custom domain as infrastructure input, for\nexample `domain: \"app.example.com\"`. Keep the application's own\n`PUBLIC_ORIGIN` bound to `Cloudflare.Worker.URL`; do not repeat that domain in\nits environment or build `\"https://\" + domain` there. Aliases and incoming\nrequest origins are separate from the host's configured canonical address.\n\nFor a different host, use that resource's native Output, such as `api.url`.\nPass the Output to the receiving resource; check its resolved value with the\nowning Schema when application code reads the binding. If the native address\ncan be absent, preserve that absence as `null` for an env binding and reject it\nwith the Schema. Do not replace an absent Output with a guessed address.\n\nKeep expected validation failures typed. Beta.79's `Output.mapEffect` callback\naccepts no expected errors, so do not force Schema decoding into it with\n`Effect.orDie`. `Output.map` may select a checked resource attribute or represent\nabsence; it must not reconstruct the hostname. Resolve and decode Outputs\nseparately before writing workflow or receipt values.\n\nCheck the selected Alchemy version's self URL, Vite, native Worker and local\nprovider APIs before copying this example. See the\n[matching primary sources](../sources.md#cloudflare-website-origins).\nLocal source/type checks do not prove live domain routing.\n\n## Separate Website and native API Workers\n\nUse standard Website.Vite for the frontend and a native Effect Worker for the\nAPI. The root stack deploys both with exact same-stage service/public bindings.\nPreview has its own API; a Website preview parent does not authorise using the\nProduction API. Do not wrap TanStack in a backend Worker or recreate an API\nruntime around the native incoming request fibre.\n\nCheck the installed Alchemy build/runtime mechanism before reading deployment\nservices in a Worker declaration. The qualified beta.79 runtime build guard\nremoves stack-only requirements from the uploaded entry. Test the real native\nentry without those services, return from startup, then request and release\nresources. A typecheck or successful upload alone does not prove startup.\n\nTest browser CORS with the native client's actual headers and an actual browser\ncall. Tracing headers b3 and traceparent are part of the qualified client's\nrequest; disabling tracing to avoid preflight is not a correction. Separate\nremote MCP Origin validation from matching-origin Website WebMCP registration.\n\n## Secrets\n\nPrefer provider-managed secret references or Secrets Store bindings over plaintext workflow values.\n\n- Acquire credentials through redacted Config.\n- Validate secret binding names and stage.\n- Never emit values in plans, logs, outputs, or receipts.\n- Separate credential issuance from resource apply.\n- Read back metadata or binding presence, never the secret.\n- Revoke disposable credentials after lifecycle proof.\n\n## Observability\n\nCloudflare platform observability and application telemetry are separate sources.\n\nFor Cloudflare-native logs or destinations:\n\n- identify Worker/service/environment/stage;\n- define retention and sampling;\n- read back the destination/binding;\n- query the downstream provider.\n\nFor Effect OTLP export:\n\n- keep endpoint/token in a redacted binding;\n- decode the binding at application ingress;\n- attach one exporter Layer to the runtime;\n- preserve `waitUntil` or the host completion mechanism;\n- test transport wiring;\n"},"started_at_ms":1791132002254,"completed_at_ms":1791132002254}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-04T16:40:02.313Z","ordinal":15100,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a107c9-6808-7933-b9e2-783598122435","call_id":"call_eACNWo7n7NWlep5YeOYj9l0J","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"b5d721\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000005792,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":6242,\"output\":\"export interface PlatformProps {\\n  /**\\n   * @internal type used to signal when this is an effect-native implementation\\n   * @default false\\n   */\\n  isExternal?: boolean;\\n}\\n\\n/**\\n * Provide the platform class's layer (`cls.make(props, impl)`) with a\\n * lifetime that matches the phase.\\n *\\n * **At runtime** (`__ALCHEMY_RUNTIME__`, folded to `true` in every bundled\\n * artifact) the layer builds against the AMBIENT scope. `Effect.provide` is\\n * implemented with `scopedWith`, so its transient region scope would tear\\n * the layer down — firing init-level finalizers and releasing\\n * `Layer.scoped` services — the moment init completes. The runtime bridges\\n * evaluate the entrypoint under the instance-lifetime build scope (closed\\n * at instance shutdown where the platform offers one — Lambda's SIGTERM\\n * window — and never on workerd), so building against it keeps instance\\n * services alive for the instance.\\n *\\n * **At plan/deploy** it stays `Effect.provide`: the transient region evicts\\n * the layer's memo entry when each `yield*` of the class completes, so\\n * every deploy in a session re-evaluates the resource and re-registers its\\n * Output sources. Building on the session scope would keep the memo alive\\n * across deploys and a second `stack.deploy` would skip source\\n * registration (`MissingSourceError`).\\n */\\nconst provideClassLayer =\\n  <ROut, E2, RIn>(layer: Layer.Layer<ROut, E2, RIn>) =>\\n  <A, E, R>(\\n    self: Effect.Effect<A, E, R>,\\n  ): Effect.Effect<A, E | E2, RIn | Scope | Exclude<R, ROut>> =>\\n    (globalThis.__ALCHEMY_RUNTIME__\\n      ? Effect.flatMap(Effect.scope, (scope) =>\\n          Effect.flatMap(Layer.buildWithScope(layer, scope), (context) =>\\n            Effect.provideContext(self, context),\\n          ),\\n        )\\n      : Effect.provide(self, layer)) as Effect.Effect<\\n      A,\\n      E | E2,\\n      RIn | Scope | Exclude<R, ROut>\\n    >;\\n\\nexport type Main<InitServices = never> = void | {\\n  fetch?:\\n    | HttpEffect<InitServices | PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope>\\n    | Effect.Effect<\\n        HttpEffect<InitServices | PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope>,\\n        never,\\n        InitServices | PlatformServices\\n      >;\\n};\\n\\nexport interface MainRpc<Req = never> {\\n  [key: string]:\\n    | Effect.Effect<\\n        any,\\n        any,\\n        PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | HttpServerRequest | Scope | Req\\n      >\\n    | Stream.Stream<\\n        any,\\n        any,\\n        PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | HttpServerRequest | Scope | Req\\n      >\\n    | ((\\n        ...args: any[]\\n      ) =>\\n        | Effect.Effect<\\n            any,\\n            any,\\n            PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope | Req\\n          >\\n        | Stream.Stream<\\n            any,\\n            any,\\n            PlatformServices | RuntimeContext | Scope | Req\\n          >);\\n}\\n\\n// Strip `void`/`undefined`/`never` from `Shape` before intersecting it with\\n// `BaseShape`. This matters when `Shape` fails its `extends MainShape`\\n// constraint (e.g. a `fetch` handler that leaks an error): TS clamps `Shape`\\n// to the constraint union `void | { fetch: ... }`, and a *distributive*\\n// conditional would split that into `BaseShape | ({ fetch } & BaseShape)` — a\\n// union. Feeding a union into the `new (_: never): ...` construct signature\\n// makes the base class a union type, which surfaces as the cryptic\\n// ts(2509) \\\"Base constructor return type ... is not an object type\\\" instead of\\n// the real assignability error on the `impl` argument. Excluding `void` here\\n// keeps the construct-sig return a single object type, so only the actionable\\n// error remains.\\nexport type MakeShape<Shape, BaseShape> = [\\n  Exclude<Shape, void | undefined>,\\n] extends [never]\\n  ? Exclude<BaseShape, void | undefined>\\n  : Exclude<Shape, void | undefined> & Exclude<BaseShape, void | undefined>;\\n\\n// Services provided to the Resource's init/props effects. Deliberately does\\n// NOT include `Scope`: init runs once per instance under a build scope that\\n// closes at instance shutdown at best (Lambda's SIGTERM window; never on\\n// workerd), so init code that needs a scope must not typecheck. Handlers get\\n// a fresh per-event `Scope` from the bridge — note the explicit `| Scope` on\\n// the handler positions in `Main` / `MainRpc` above.\\nexport type PlatformServices =\\n  | NodeServices\\n  | HttpClient\\n  | Provider<any>\\n  | ProviderCollectionLike\\n  | Stack\\n  | StackServices\\n  | Stage;\\n\\n/** A platform declaration's logical identity, readable without yielding it. */\\nexport interface PlatformIdentity<Id extends string = string> {\\n  readonly LogicalId: Id;\\n}\\n\\n    createRuntimeContext: (id: string) => BaseRuntimeContext;\\n    /**\\n     * Legacy type names this platform's resource was previously registered\\n     * under (see `ResourceOptions.aliases`) — threaded to the underlying\\n     * `Resource` so state persisted under a pre-rename type keeps\\n     * resolving.\\n     */\\n    aliases?: string[];\\n    // `onCreate` runs inside the resource-construction context, which already\\n    // carries the Stack's providers — so the hook may yield child resources\\n    // (e.g. an async Worker registering a `WorkflowResource` for a bound\\n    // Workflow). Allow an ambient requirement (`any`) rather than forcing\\n    // `never`; it is discharged by the surrounding provider context.\\n    onCreate?: (resource: R, props: any) => Effect.Effect<void, never, any>;\\n    /**\\n     * Transform the user-supplied props before the underlying resource is\\n     * declared. Runs in the resource-construction context (Stack, Namespace,\\n     * and providers are available), so the hook may compose REAL child\\n     * resources (the `StaticSite` pattern) and return derived props that\\n     * reference their Outputs. Implementations MUST be a no-op at runtime\\n     * (guard on `globalThis.__ALCHEMY_RUNTIME__`) — the hook is evaluated\\n     * wherever the props effect is, including inside deployed bundles.\\n     */\\n    transformProps?: (id: string, props: any) => Effect.Effect<any, any, any>;\\n  },\\n  methods?: { [key: string]: any },\\n): any => {\\n  type Props = any;\\n  type Impl = Effect.Effect<any>;\\n\\n  // Platform registrations must have resolved props by plan time: every\\n  // legitimate construction (a `.make(props, impl)` Layer build, a tag\\n  // declared with props, a plain call) produces them, so props still\\n  // `undefined` at plan can only be a bare-tag forward reference whose\\n  // `.make` Layer was never provided — `Plan.make` fails fast naming the\\n  // class and its Layer (#1054).\\n  const resource = Resource(type, {\\n    aliases: hooks.aliases,\\n    requiresImplementation: true,\\n  });\\n  const PlatformContext = RuntimeContext;\\n\\n  // Apply the optional `transformProps` hook to a (possibly Effect-valued)\\n  // props argument. Returns the props untouched when no hook is installed so\\n  // the plain-object fast paths below keep working.\\n  const applyTransformProps = (id: string, props: any): any =>\\n    hooks.transformProps === undefined\\n      ? props\\n      : Effect.flatMap(\\n          Effect.isEffect(props)\\n            ? (props as Effect.Effect<any>)\\n          Self,\\n          Effect.flatMap(\\n            Effect.all([\\n              (() => {\\n                const transformed = applyTransformProps(id, props);\\n                return Effect.isEffect(transformed)\\n                  ? (transformed as Effect.Effect<any>)\\n                  : Effect.succeed(transformed ?? {});\\n              })(),\\n              Effect.sync(() => hooks.createRuntimeContext(id)),\\n              Effect.context<never>(),\\n            ]),\\n            Effect.fn(function* ([props, runtimeContext, outerServices]) {\\n              // The init effect (`impl`) is evaluated inside an\\n              // `Effect.provide(...)` region below, whose implementation\\n              // (`scopedWith`) would otherwise shadow the ambient `Scope`\\n              // with a transient one that closes the moment init returns.\\n              // Pin init's ambient scope to this layer's build scope\\n              // instead: under the runtime bridges that scope belongs to\\n              // the instance-lifetime build, so init-level finalizers run\\n              // at instance shutdown or not at all — never per event\\n              // (workerd never closes it; the Lambda entry closes it in the\\n              // SIGTERM window). Request-coupled cleanup belongs in\\n              // handlers, where the bridge provides a per-event scope.\\n              const buildScope = yield* Effect.scope;\\n              const instance = Object.assign(\\n                yield* resource(id, props as any).pipe(\\n                  Effect.flatMap(\\n                    (resource) =>\\n                      hooks\\n                        .onCreate?.(resource, props)\\n                        .pipe(Effect.map(() => resource)) ??\\n                      Effect.succeed(resource),\\n                  ),\\n                ),\\n                runtimeContext,\\n              );\\n\\n              yield* impl.pipe(\\n                Effect.flatMap((impl) => {\\n                  if (!impl) return Effect.void;\\n                  const shape = impl as Record<string, unknown>;\\n                  // Serve when there's a `fetch` handler OR any RPC shape\\n                  // methods. A pure-RPC impl (methods, no `fetch`) still needs\\n                  // the server to boot — hand `serveRpc` a default 404 fallback\\n                  // so `/__rpc__/*` is dispatched to the shape methods and\\n                  // everything else 404s.\\n                  // May be an `HttpEffect` or an Effect resolving to one (the\\n                  // `Main.fetch` shape); `serve` accepts both.\\n                  const fetch = shape.fetch as any;\\n                  const hasRpcMethods = Object.keys(shape).some(\\n                    (key) => key !== \\\"fetch\\\",\\n                  );\\n                  if (!fetch && !hasRpcMethods) return Effect.void;\\n                  // Hand the full impl to `serve` so the runtime can expose any\\n                  // non-handler methods on the impl shape (RPC methods)\\n * **Example:** Enabling Workers Cache on an async Worker\\n * ```typescript\\n * {\\n *   main: \\\"./src/worker.ts\\\",\\n *   cache: {\\n *     enabled: true,\\n *     crossVersionCache: true,\\n *   },\\n * }\\n * ```\\n *\\n * ### Background Work & Scopes\\n * Each incoming event (fetch, RPC call, scheduled run) gets its own Effect\\n * `Scope`. When the handler finishes, the bridge closes that scope and\\n * registers the close promise with workerd's `ctx.waitUntil` — so\\n * finalizers added with `Effect.addFinalizer` inside a handler run *after*\\n * the response is sent, without blocking it, and the Worker stays alive\\n * until they settle. Streaming responses transfer the scope to the stream,\\n * so those finalizers run when the stream completes instead.\\n *\\n * For ad-hoc background work, `WorkerExecutionContext.waitUntil(effect)`\\n * forks an Effect with the caller's full context and keeps the invocation\\n * alive until it settles. The context can be yielded once in the\\n * constructor and used from any handler; its methods are `RuntimeContext`-\\n * colored, so they can only run inside a handler.\\n *\\n * The constructor is evaluated once per isolate: the bridge builds the\\n * Worker's layer stack on the first event and every later event reuses the\\n * built services. Resolve services, bind resources, build handlers there —\\n * one-shot I/O that caches a plain value (e.g. fetching a secret for a\\n * client) is fine, but nothing disposable: the build scope is never closed\\n * (workerd has no isolate-teardown hook), so a finalizer added in the\\n * constructor never runs, and I/O-backed objects (sockets, response bodies) are\\n * pinned to the request that created them. Anything that needs cleanup\\n * belongs in a handler, where `Effect.addFinalizer` attaches to the\\n * per-event scope.\\n *\\n * **Example:** Post-response cleanup with a scope finalizer\\n * ```typescript\\n * return {\\n *   fetch: Effect.gen(function* () {\\n *     // runs after this response is sent, kept alive by waitUntil\\n *     yield* Effect.addFinalizer(() => flushMetrics().pipe(Effect.ignore));\\n *     return HttpServerResponse.text(\\\"ok\\\");\\n *   }),\\n * };\\n * ```\\n# Ownership and Boundaries\\n\\n## Contents\\n\\n- [Start with lifecycle ownership](#start-with-lifecycle-ownership)\\n- [Write an ownership matrix](#write-an-ownership-matrix)\\n- [Decide when Alchemy should own a resource](#decide-when-alchemy-should-own-a-resource)\\n- [Separate authority from capability](#separate-authority-from-capability)\\n- [Separate claims](#separate-claims)\\n- [Bound custom configuration of foreign resources](#bound-custom-configuration-of-foreign-resources)\\n\\n## Start with lifecycle ownership\\n\\nDo not begin with provider APIs. Begin with a resource inventory and name the system that owns each lifecycle transition.\\n\\nUse these lifecycle verbs precisely:\\n\\n- **declare**: describe desired state without claiming mutation;\\n- **create**: make a resource that does not exist;\\n- **adopt**: attach state to a pre-existing resource;\\n- **observe**: read and report without changing;\\n- **configure**: mutate a bounded setting on a resource owned elsewhere;\\n- **reconcile**: converge the managed fields to desired state;\\n- **deploy**: create a runtime or artifact from a source revision;\\n- **retain**: remove the resource from this stack without deleting it;\\n- **destroy**: delete the provider resource and verify absence.\\n\\nDo not collapse these verbs into “manage”.\\n\\n## Write an ownership matrix\\n\\nBefore implementation, record:\\n\\n| Resource            | Lifecycle owner                   | Deployment owner | State owner   | Readback authority | Removal policy |\\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------- | ------------------ | -------------- |\\n| Cloudflare Worker   | Alchemy or Vercel Git, choose one | named workflow   | named backend | Cloudflare API     | delete/retain  |\\n| Vercel deployment   | Vercel Git by default             | Vercel Git       | Vercel        | Vercel API         | Vercel policy  |\\n| Axiom dataset       | Alchemy                           | not applicable   | Alchemy state | Axiom API/query    | retain/delete  |\\n| WorkOS redirect URI | WorkOS or narrow custom resource  | not applicable   | explicit      | WorkOS API         | delete/retain  |\\n\\nAdd account, organisation, project, tenant, zone, or team identifiers when ambiguity is possible. Store safe identifiers, never credentials.\\n\\n## Decide when Alchemy should own a resource\\n\\nUse Alchemy when all relevant conditions hold:\\n\\n- the repository owns the desired state;\\n- the lifecycle can be expressed deterministically;\\n- independent readback exists;\\n- create/update/delete semantics are understood;\\n- retries can converge;\\n- partial failure can be recovered;\\n- stage identity can prevent collisions;\\n- credentials can be scoped to the authorised operation;\\n- the repository can test the graph and the provider result.\\n\\nPrefer another owner or no new IaC when:\\n\\n- Vercel Git already owns deployments and source provenance;\\n- a resource is manually governed for a documented reason;\\n- the provider API cannot support safe read, update, or delete semantics;\\n- the only available credential is materially over-broad;\\n- the repository cannot distinguish its resource from a foreign one;\\n- live state cannot be read independently;\\n- the proposed module exists only to make the tree look symmetrical.\\n\\nAn explicit “no Alchemy role” architecture decision is stronger than an unused scaffold.\\n\\n## Separate authority from capability\\n\\nA credential that can mutate a provider does not authorise every mutation it permits.\\n\\nRequire an operation-specific authority record:\\n\\n- operation: plan, create, update, adopt, reconcile, destroy, issue token;\\n- stage and environment;\\n- exact resource set;\\n- expected source revision;\\n- credential provenance;\\n- allowed replacement/deletion;\\n- expiry or single-use constraint where appropriate;\\n- required preflight and readback;\\n- rollback or recovery owner.\\n\\nKeep provider discovery and read-only diagnostics within their stated scope. Do not escalate from “inspect” to “apply”.\\n\\n## Separate claims\\n\\nKeep these claims independent:\\n\\n1. **source claim** — which repository/ref/revision defines desired state;\\n2. **graph claim** — what the evaluated Alchemy stack intends;\\n3. **state claim** — what Alchemy state records;\\n4. **provider claim** — what the provider independently reports;\\n5. **deployment claim** — which source artifact a runtime serves;\\n6. **Preview claim** — behaviour in the Preview environment;\\n7. **Production claim** — behaviour in Production;\\n8. **public claim** — what an external caller actually observes.\\n\\nNever use one claim as shorthand for another. For example:\\n\\n- a green deployment job does not prove the public asset path;\\n- a resource in state does not prove it still exists;\\n- an HTTP 200 does not prove the expected redirect inventory;\\n- an accepted telemetry request does not prove data is queryable;\\n- a branch deployment does not prove default-branch establishment.\\n\\n## Bound custom configuration of foreign resources\\n\\nIf Alchemy configures a resource deployed elsewhere:\\n\\n- name the foreign deployment owner;\\n- manage only a closed field set;\\n- read the current value before writing;\\n- compute a semantic diff;\\n- reject unrelated changes;\\n- use retain by default;\\n- read back the exact field after write;\\n- never claim deployment provenance.\\n\\nThis pattern suits narrowly governed environment variables, redirect URIs, or observability drains. It does not justify wrapping an entire provider client.\\n# Cloudflare\\n\\n## Contents\\n\\n- [Ownership decisions](#ownership-decisions)\\n- [File ownership](#file-ownership)\\n- [Worker and asset semantics](#worker-and-asset-semantics)\\n- [Routes zones and domains](#routes-zones-and-domains)\\n- [Bindings and storage](#bindings-and-storage)\\n- [Website origin rules](#website-origin-rules)\\n- [Secrets](#secrets)\\n- [Observability](#observability)\\n- [State](#state)\\n- [Readback checklist](#readback-checklist)\\n\\n## Ownership decisions\\n\\nDeclare which system owns each Cloudflare lifecycle:\\n\\n- Worker or static-asset deployment;\\n- routes and custom domains;\\n- DNS/zone configuration;\\n- R2, D1, KV, Durable Objects, Queues, or Secrets Store;\\n- environment secrets and bindings;\\n- logs, metrics, traces, and observability destinations;\\n- remote state infrastructure.\\n\\nDo not assume Alchemy owns all Cloudflare resources because it owns one Worker.\\n\\n## File ownership\\n\\nFor a small graph:\\n\\n```text\\nsrc/lib/build/cloudflare.ts\\n```\\n\\nFor a substantial graph:\\n\\n```text\\nsrc/lib/build/cloudflare/\\n  stack.ts\\n  website.ts\\n  worker.ts\\n  storage.ts\\n  routes.ts\\n  secrets.ts\\n  observability.ts\\n```\\n\\nKeep stage/config Schemas outside provider declarations when other providers share them.\\n\\n## Worker and asset semantics\\n\\nMake asset routing policy explicit. In particular, distinguish asset-first and Worker-first behaviour and test the intended choice. A deployed Worker and green CI do not prove that static assets return the correct content type or bypass the Worker.\\n\\nTest:\\n\\n- root document;\\n- hashed CSS and JavaScript assets;\\n- missing assets;\\n- API paths;\\n- redirects;\\n- cache headers;\\n- content types;\\n- route precedence.\\n\\nRecord compatibility date, compatibility flags, runtime limits, module format, and bundler inputs in a stable typed owner. Keep application and deployment configuration aligned through one contract or an invariant test.\\n\\n## Routes, zones, and domains\\n\\nTreat a zone or domain as foreign unless this repository creates and owns it.\\n\\n- Read the target zone before applying routes.\\n- Adopt only under explicit authority.\\n- Default adopted zones and routes to retain.\\n- Restrict Production route adoption to the Production stage.\\n- Reject a route whose account/zone does not match the authority record.\\n- Read back the route pattern and bound Worker after apply.\\n- Prove the public host independently.\\n\\nDo not infer route correctness from a Worker URL.\\n\\n## Bindings and storage\\n\\nModel each binding as typed desired state:\\n\\n- binding name;\\n- resource kind;\\n- physical resource identity;\\n- stage/environment;\\n- creation/adoption policy;\\n- removal policy.\\n\\nAssert that application code and deployment configuration use the same binding names. For Durable Objects, include class name, migration/tag policy, and deployment sequencing. For R2/D1/KV, make data retention explicit and default destructive deletion to forbidden until authorised.\\n\\n## Website origin rules\\n\\nAn origin is the protocol and host, with an optional port, such as\\n`http://127.0.0.1:3210`. The deployed host owns that address. Applications must\\nnever hard-code it or build it from a stage, Worker name, account subdomain,\\ncustom domain or development port.\\n\\nFor Alchemy `2.0.0-beta.79`, use the native self URL binding on the host that\\nneeds its own address:\\n\\n```typescript\\nconst website =\\n  yield *\\n  Cloudflare.Website.Vite(\\\"Website\\\", {\\n    rootDir: \\\"apps/web\\\",\\n    env: { PUBLIC_ORIGIN: Cloudflare.Worker.URL },\\n  });\\n\\n// The same binding works in a native Worker declaration.\\nconst api =\\n  yield *\\n  Cloudflare.Worker(\\\"Api\\\", {\\n    main: \\\"./src/worker.ts\\\",\\n    env: { PUBLIC_ORIGIN: Cloudflare.Worker.URL },\\n  });\\n```\\n\\nAlchemy resolves this binding to the local development proxy during local\\ndevelopment, the deployed `workers.dev` address, or the configured custom\\ndomain. Do not add separate local/cloud address formulas or a copied-address\\nfallback. For Vite browser code, a `VITE_`-prefixed self URL binding uses the\\nsame native resolution before the client bundle is built.\\n\\nIt is valid to declare a chosen custom domain as infrastructure input, for\\nexample `domain: \\\"app.example.com\\\"`. Keep the application's own\\n`PUBLIC_ORIGIN` bound to `Cloudflare.Worker.URL`; do not repeat that domain in\\nits environment or build `\\\"https://\\\" + domain` there. Aliases and incoming\\nrequest origins are separate from the host's configured canonical address.\\n\\nFor a different host, use that resource's native Output, such as `api.url`.\\nPass the Output to the receiving resource; check its resolved value with the\\nowning Schema when application code reads the binding. If the native address\\ncan be absent, preserve that absence as `null` for an env binding and reject it\\nwith the Schema. Do not replace an absent Output with a guessed address.\\n\\nKeep expected validation failures typed. Beta.79's `Output.mapEffect` callback\\naccepts no expected errors, so do not force Schema decoding into it with\\n`Effect.orDie`. `Output.map` may select a checked resource attribute or represent\\nabsence; it must not reconstruct the hostname. Resolve and decode Outputs\\nseparately before writing workflow or receipt values.\\n\\nCheck the selected Alchemy version's self URL, Vite, native Worker and local\\nprovider APIs before copying this example. See the\\n[matching primary sources](../sources.md#cloudflare-website-origins).\\nLocal source/type checks do not prove live domain routing.\\n\\n## Separate Website and native API Workers\\n\\nUse standard Website.Vite for the frontend and a native Effect Worker for the\\nAPI. The root stack deploys both with exact same-stage service/public bindings.\\nPreview has its own API; a Website preview parent does not authorise using the\\nProduction API. Do not wrap TanStack in a backend Worker or recreate an API\\nruntime around the native incoming request fibre.\\n\\nCheck the installed Alchemy build/runtime mechanism before reading deployment\\nservices in a Worker declaration. The qualified beta.79 runtime build guard\\nremoves stack-only requirements from the uploaded entry. Test the real native\\nentry without those services, return from startup, then request and release\\nresources. A typecheck or successful upload alone does not prove startup.\\n\\nTest browser CORS with the native client's actual headers and an actual browser\\ncall. Tracing headers b3 and traceparent are part of the qualified client's\\nrequest; disabling tracing to avoid preflight is not a correction. Separate\\nremote MCP Origin validation from matching-origin Website WebMCP registration.\\n\\n## Secrets\\n\\nPrefer provider-managed secret references or Secrets Store bindings over plaintext workflow values.\\n\\n- Acquire credentials through redacted Config.\\n- Validate secret binding names and stage.\\n- Never emit values in plans, logs, outputs, or receipts.\\n- Separate credential issuance from resource apply.\\n- Read back metadata or binding presence, never the secret.\\n- Revoke disposable credentials after lifecycle proof.\\n\\n## Observability\\n\\nCloudflare platform observability and application telemetry are separate sources.\\n\\nFor Cloudflare-native logs or destinations:\\n\\n- identify Worker/service/environment/stage;\\n- define retention and sampling;\\n- read back the destination/binding;\\n- query the downstream provider.\\n\\nFor Effect OTLP export:\\n\\n- keep endpoint/token in a redacted binding;\\n- decode the binding at application ingress;\\n- attach one exporter Layer to the runtime;\\n- preserve `waitUntil` or the host completion mechanism;\\n- test transport wiring;\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1055e-488d-75e2-ab79-097882eda37b","create_time":1791132002.312996}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
